  "generated_text": "There are 50 integers between 20 and 69, inclusive.  We can choose 5 of them in $\\binom{50}{5}$ ways.\n\nTo choose 5 integers with different tens digits, we can choose 5 tens digits from 2 to 6, and then choose 1 integer from each tens digit.  There are $\\binom{5}{5}$ ways to choose the tens digits, and then $10^5$ ways to choose one integer from each tens digit.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the 5 integers all have different tens digits is $\\frac{\\binom{5}{5} \\cdot 10^5}{\\binom{50}{5}} = \\frac{10^5}{\\binom{50}{5}} = \\frac{10^5}{\\frac{50!}{5! \\cdot 45!}} = \\frac{10^5 \\cdot 5! \\cdot 45!}{50!} = \\frac{10^5 \\cdot 120}{50!} = \\boxed{0.02}$.\nThe answer is: 0.02",
